## Why This Is Asked

Probes production-grade multi-tenant routing with cost control, latency targets, RBAC, and integration with Azure services (Cosmos DB, AD, Cognitive Search, Durable Functions, API Management). Assesses ability to design end-to-end data flow, observability, and testing strategies.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy and quota management in Cosmos DB
- Multi-model routing: cost-aware model choice + RAG grounding
- Latency budgets and backpressure via Durable Functions
- RBAC via Azure AD and API Management gating
- Grounding via Cognitive Search KB

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing decision
function chooseEngine(policies, tenantId, workload) {
  const p = policies[tenantId];
  if (!p) return 'GPT-3.5-turbo';
  if (workload.latencyMs > p.latencyBudget) return p.defaultModel;
  if (workload.costPerRequest > p.costBudget) return 'GPT-3.5-turbo';
  return p.preferredModel;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model tenant quotas in Cosmos DB for fairness during bursts?
- What tests validate SLA adherence under canary deployments?
- How would you implement backpressure across regional deployments and API gateways?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical security-first design for Azure OpenAI with code-gen, focusing on secret redaction, key vault integration, tenant isolation, and auditable logging.

## Key Concepts
- Secret management with Azure Key Vault and Managed Identity
- Request/response redaction and policy enforcement
- Data isolation per tenant and private networking
- End-to-end auditing in Purview
- Testing: redaction fuzzing and integration tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: redact secrets before sending to OpenAI
function redact(input, keys){
  let out = input;
  keys.forEach(k => { out = out.replace(new RegExp(k, 'g'), 'REDACTED'); });
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift in redaction rules across tenants?
- What data plane logs would you guard and what retention policy would you apply?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes architectural decisions for multi-tenant Azure AI deployments with strict data residency, governance, and explainability requirements. It tests ability to plan region-aware routing, tenant-scoped knowledge bases, identity boundaries, and end-to-end auditing.

## Key Concepts

- Data residency and customer-managed keys (CMK) for storage
- Region-local Cognitive Search indexes per tenant
- Region-aware model routing for Azure OpenAI
- Per-tenant RBAC using Azure AD
- Explainability logging to Data Lake with traceability
- End-to-end testing including latency, privacy, and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo region routing for tenants
function getTenantRegion(tenantId) {
  const regionMap = {
    't1': 'eastus',
    't2': 'northeurope'
  };
  return regionMap[tenantId] || 'eastus';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate cross-region consistency and prove data residency?
- What failure modes require circuit breakers and fallback paths, and how would you implement them?

## Why This Is Asked
Explores runtime policy update, data residency, and per-tenant isolation without redeploying models. 

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy engine integrated with config store
- Per-tenant data sovereignty and RBAC
- Latency targets and fallback paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: policy fetch & eval before OpenAI call
async function handle(req, tenantId){
  const policies = await fetchPolicy(tenantId);
  if(!evaluate(policies, req)) throw new Error('Policy violation');
  const redacted = redact(req.body);
  return await callOpenAI(redacted);
}
```

